WEBVTT

00:00:00.672 --> 00:00:02.315
Coding agents are fast.

00:00:02.586 --> 00:00:04.099
Managing them isn't.

00:00:04.547 --> 00:00:09.273
This is AI Squared: one native workspace
for your coding agents, your

00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:11.112
projects, and your machines.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:14.938
It starts with one terminal.

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:16.109
Then four.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.464
Then twelve.

00:00:17.938 --> 00:00:19.634
Which one is still working?

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:21.834
Which one finished an hour ago?

00:00:22.131 --> 00:00:25.832
And which one has been waiting on a yes or
no question since lunch?

00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:28.280
A quiet terminal tells you nothing.

00:00:28.538 --> 00:00:33.134
So AI Squared gives every agent a
reporting channel, and shows you who is

00:00:33.134 --> 00:00:35.989
busy, who is waiting, and who is done.

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:38.909
Agents are hungry.

00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:43.362
Builds, tests and local models will bury a
single computer.

00:00:43.814 --> 00:00:45.340
So spread the team out.

00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:49.816
Link the same project across your
workstation, your build server, and your

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:50.561
laptop.

00:00:51.003 --> 00:00:53.219
Then drive all of it from one seat.

00:00:53.516 --> 00:00:58.607
Start, stop, watch, and type into remote
terminals, over an encrypted

00:00:58.607 --> 00:00:59.274
workgroup.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.570
Close the window, and agents keep working
while AI Squared runs in the

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:05.393
background.

00:01:06.997 --> 00:01:09.513
Everything is organized the way you think.

00:01:09.784 --> 00:01:13.133
A project holds a team of named agents.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:18.469
Each agent has its own sessions, its own
history, and its own machine.

00:01:18.924 --> 00:01:20.425
Need isolation?

00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:22.847
Give an agent its own Git worktree.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.893
Up to sixteen agents per project, grouped
by project or by machine.

00:01:29.531 --> 00:01:31.578
Bring the agents you already use.

00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:37.021
Claude Code, Codex and Grok run in real
terminals, exactly as they do today.

00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:42.542
AI Squared connects each one to its MCP
server at launch, so they report

00:01:42.542 --> 00:01:45.787
progress, ask questions, and tell you when
they are done.

00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:49.721
They can even screenshot the app they just
built, and put it on your Launch

00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:50.221
button.

00:01:51.839 --> 00:01:53.639
Prefer to keep it on your own hardware?

00:01:53.935 --> 00:01:58.391
ai2code is the built-in agent that runs on
local models.

00:01:58.809 --> 00:02:03.119
Browse models from Hugging Face, and
estimate what fits your GPU before

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:03.929
downloading.

00:02:04.402 --> 00:02:07.842
Or connect an OpenAI-compatible model
server.

00:02:08.266 --> 00:02:12.758
And chat with Johnny Five, using your
selected model, with optional web

00:02:12.758 --> 00:02:13.386
search.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.700
Give your project a project type: a
reusable Markdown brief.

00:02:20.146 --> 00:02:22.011
It tells the agent how you build.

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:25.761
Your layout, your tools, your testing
rules, your style.

00:02:26.221 --> 00:02:30.518
Describe what you want, press Generate,
and the agent starts with the full

00:02:30.518 --> 00:02:31.341
context.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.074
Now the most powerful part: the Pipeline.

00:02:36.562 --> 00:02:38.244
Save your prompts as a queue.

00:02:38.541 --> 00:02:41.604
Choose an agent and a coding tool for each
task.

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:45.728
Press Automate to run the queue on the
project's owning machine.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.306
Each task starts a fresh session.

00:02:48.603 --> 00:02:52.848
An explicit completion report triggers
your milestone prompt, which commits

00:02:52.848 --> 00:02:54.400
the changes by default.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.201
Agents add to the queue themselves, when
they find a bug outside their task.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:03.948
Recurring tasks run on a schedule while AI
Squared stays open in the

00:03:03.948 --> 00:03:04.758
background.

00:03:05.168 --> 00:03:06.329
Queue the work tonight.

00:03:06.574 --> 00:03:08.230
Review the results in the morning.

00:03:08.527 --> 00:03:12.837
Completion reports can include failed
tests, so check the results before you

00:03:12.837 --> 00:03:13.387
ship.

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:17.592
Your agents are a team, so let them talk.

00:03:18.015 --> 00:03:23.031
In Agent Chat, ask Hannibal to build it,
and BA to document it.

00:03:23.314 --> 00:03:26.989
They message each other, across machines,
and report back to you.

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:32.478
Personas give each agent a face, a role, a
skill file, and even a

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.197
voice.

00:03:34.804 --> 00:03:35.757
There is more.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:39.208
Review file changes since an agent session
began.

00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:43.524
Drag a box around exactly what you mean,
and hand the screenshot to your

00:03:43.524 --> 00:03:44.204
agent.

00:03:44.488 --> 00:03:46.925
It is a fast way to polish an interface.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.639
Record the session as a time-lapse video,
with your webcam in the corner.

00:03:52.072 --> 00:03:56.812
And, with the speech engine configured,
hear questions read aloud in a

00:03:56.812 --> 00:03:57.986
custom voice.

00:03:59.588 --> 00:04:00.802
AI Squared.

00:04:01.046 --> 00:04:01.908
One seat.

00:04:02.179 --> 00:04:03.198
Every agent.

00:04:03.468 --> 00:04:04.435
Every machine.

